"Troubleshooting an Intrusion Detection Dataset: the CICIDS2017 Case Study | Home (https://intrusion-detection.distrinet-research.be/WTMC2021/)\nciteturn14view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://intrusion-detection.distrinet-research.be/WTMC2021/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 36\nL0:   * cite0†Home L1:   * cite1†Extended Documentation L2:   * cite2†Tools & Dataset L3:   * cite3†Paper L4:   * cite4†Contact L5: \nL6: # Troubleshooting an Intrusion Detection Dataset: the CICIDS2017 Case Study\nL7: \nL8: Gints Engelen, Vera Rimmer and Wouter Joosen\nL9: ## UPDATE (18/11/2022): For the most recent version of CICIDS2017 (improved ground-truth labelling and additional features) as well as a fixed version of CSECICIDS2018, please check out cite5†our latest work here. L10: \nL11: This page serves as a complementary material to our paper published at cite6†WTMC 2021†wtmc.info , which can be found cite3†here .\nL12:   * For ML and security practitioners who want to use the fixed CICFlowMeter tool for feature extraction from traffic, the improved version of the CICIDS2017 dataset, or our labelling code, please head over to cite2†Tools & Dataset .\nL13:   * For the reader who is interested in the extended documentation of our paper, where we describe the findings in full, please head over to cite1†Extended Documentation .\nL14:   * For questions and suggestions, feel free to cite4†contact us by email.\nL15: ## Paper abstract\nL16: Numerous studies have demonstrated the effectiveness of machine learning techniques in application to network intrusion detection. And yet, the adoption of machine learning for securing large-scale network environments remains challenging. The community acknowledges that network security presents unique challenges for machine learning, and the lack of training data representative of modern traffic remains one of the most intractable issues.\nL17: New attempts are continuously made to develop high quality benchmark datasets and proper data collection methodologies. The CICIDS2017 dataset is one of the recent results, created to meet the demanding criterion of representativeness for network intrusion detection.\nL18: In this paper we revisit CICIDS2017 and its data collection pipeline and analyze correctness, validity and overall utility of the dataset for the learning task. During this in-depth analysis, we uncover a series of problems with traffic generation, flow construction, feature extraction and labelling that severely affect the aforementioned properties. We investigate the causes of these shortcomings and address most of them by applying an improved data processing methodology.\nL19: As a result, more than 20 percent of original traffic traces are reconstructed or relabelled. Machine learning benchmarks on the final dataset demonstrate significant improvements. Our study exemplifies how data collection issues may have enormous impact on model evaluation and provides recommendations for their anticipation and prevention.\nL20: ### BibTeX Citation\nL21: \nL22: @inproceedings{engelen2021troubleshooting,\nL23: title={Troubleshooting an Intrusion Detection Dataset: the CICIDS2017 Case Study},\nL24: author={Engelen, Gints and Rimmer, Vera and Joosen, Wouter},\nL25: booktitle={2021 IEEE Security and Privacy Workshops (SPW)},\nL26: pages={7--12},\nL27: year={2021},\nL28: organization={IEEE}\nL29: }\nL30: ### IEEE Format Reference\nL31: \nL32: G. Engelen, V. Rimmer and W. Joosen, \"Troubleshooting an Intrusion Detection Dataset: the CICIDS2017 Case Study,\" 2021 IEEE Security and Privacy Workshops (SPW), 2021, pp. 7-12, doi: 10.1109/SPW53761.2021.00009.\nL33: ## Acknowledgements\nL34: \nL35: We thank Jin Li for providing us with the labelling code. We also thank Lieven Desmet for proofreading the paper and for his helpful insights, and the reviewers for the careful examination of our work and valuable suggestions. This 